[Matthew laughing]
I would spend 14,

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:48.470
actually more, probably 16 hours a day,

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:49.700
helping them along in their life,

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:52.337
taking away their daughters,
which is how we count.

00:10:52.337 --> 00:10:54.320
- You know, David, if you love something,

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:56.280
you really shouldn't
take its children away.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:57.780
- Yeah.
- That's pretty messed up.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.270
- That's true, if you leave the daughters,

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.870
unfortunately there's about 5 million

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:04.280
of them by the end of the experiment.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:06.330
And you can't find the
original mother, that's-

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:07.861
- So, these things, they
divide and they expand

00:11:07.861 --> 00:11:09.190
and they expand.

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:10.160
- Exponentially, right?

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:11.330
So, you've got to get
rid of those daughters.

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.680
You don't kill the daughters,
you just move them away.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.620
But what we found was that
when you reduce the amount

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.970
of sugar in the plate,
so they eat glucose.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:19.263
This is what we gave them.

00:11:19.263 --> 00:11:24.263
2% glucose makes them live
about 25 divisions, daughters.

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:26.560
If you restrict that down to 0.5 glucose,

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:29.820
So, you are down to 25%
of that concentration,

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:31.670
they will live over 30.

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:33.080
And, that's really cool, right?

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.450
That you can, just by
changing the glucose levels,

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.270
and we'll get to how that
works in humans, very similar,

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:39.230
you can extend life span.

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:41.210
And what we showed was
that there's a set of genes

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:42.980
that controls that process.

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:45.633
This isn't just glucose hurting the cell.

00:11:45.633 --> 00:11:50.320
There's a genetic pathway that
gets triggered by low energy.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:52.950
And these genes, we talked
about in last episode,

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:56.520
they called "sirtuins" and
there are five of these genes

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:58.610
in yeast and seven in our bodies.

00:11:58.610 --> 00:12:00.100
And they respond to low energy.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:02.200
They're respond to other stresses as well,

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:05.423
such as high heat, low amino acids,

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:07.700
high salt.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:09.683
The sirtuins will get activated by these,

00:12:09.683 --> 00:12:12.850
what we call hormetic effects.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:14.890
What doesn't kill them,
makes them stronger.

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:17.980
And in this case, low
energy led to the activation

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:19.210
of these enzymes called sirtuins.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:23.050
And then they took care of the DNA repair

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:25.690
and also stabilization of the epigenome.

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:27.133
Now-
- There's a lot there,

00:12:28.397 --> 00:12:30.600
let's slow this down and pace this out.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:31.440
Let's start with sirtuins.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:35.190
What's actually happening when,

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:37.490
in the case of yeast or
any organism in this case,

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:39.940
because it's very similar
mechanisms, right?

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:41.920
What's actually happening with sirtuins,

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:45.180
when yeast or any other model organism

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:47.340
is calorie restricted, is fasted.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:49.970
- Yeah, well their role
is to extend the lifespan.

00:12:49.970 --> 00:12:51.940
So, what they're doing down

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:53.990
at the very minute part of the cell,

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:55.330
is they're protecting DNA

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:58.320
and making sure genes stay
"on" when they should be "on",

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.020
but what's happening
to boost their activity

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:03.793
during caloric restriction,
this low glucose level.

00:13:03.793 --> 00:13:06.360
We managed to figure out in
the first couple of years,

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.840
when I moved my lab to
Harvard Medical School,

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.200
well, started my lab at
Harvard Medical School.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:16.110
We found that low energy
activates a certain gene.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:17.550
It has a name, it's called PNC-1.

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:21.340
We have an equivalent gene
in our bodies called NAMPT

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:24.810
and turning on that PNC-1
gene in yeast was able

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:29.640
to activate the sirtuins
because that gene makes a fuel

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:31.363
for the sirtuins called NAD.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.770
And so in our bodies as
well, when we're hungry,

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.120
these genes come on that make more NAD,

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:41.740
this very small molecule that
the sirtuins need for fuel.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:43.330
And then they do the protection.

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:46.613
They repair DNA and they
stabilize the epigenome.

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:49.340
- And this is the beginning plays

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:52.690
that led to NAD boosters,

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:54.000
where we started going,

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.330
oh, well, if we can boost NAD
in other ways, and that's not,

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:00.450
we'll talk about boosters
in another episode,

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:02.630
but just to put that in the chronology

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:05.460
of how this revelation came about

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:07.790
and why so many people are
now taking the NR and NMN.

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:09.210
- Yeah, that came out of our lab.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:12.690
We found two ways to activate
the sirtuins synthetically.

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:15.180
One was to use the red wine molecule,

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:17.490
resveratrol plus some
other plant molecules

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:19.900
that are now used in supplement market.

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:22.870
And that was a big craze
in the 2000s from our lab.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:24.410
And then the second one, which is now,

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:26.817
probably an even bigger
craze is the NAD booster.

00:14:26.817 --> 00:14:29.660
And a lot of people are
shooting themselves up with NAD

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:32.400
or taking NAD, precursor supplements.

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:33.600
You mentioned NR and NMN,

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.600
we'll get to those in another episode.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:37.770
But you can mimic low glucose

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:41.300
in these yeast cells by giving
them these NAD boosters.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:43.570
Or you can turn on this
PNC-1 gene artificially,

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:46.000
and they live longer in all cases.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:47.440
And we found that if we deleted

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.303
that sirtuin gene in the yeast,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.920
either one of them or multiple ones,

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:57.080
then caloric restriction in
the yeast didn't work anymore,

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:58.950
which showed us, told us

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:02.050
that this wasn't just
glucose being bad for you.

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:04.530
Glucose doesn't hurt you,
it's that the low glucose

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:08.390
is activating the natural
defense state of that cell,

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:10.720
which we have inherited over the billions

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:12.150
of years, since we separated.

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:13.260
- People who are just going to cut out

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:14.450
that little part where you just say,

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:15.711
glucose, isn't bad for you.

00:15:15.711 --> 00:15:17.090
And they're just going
to play that on a loop

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.471
and it's going to be great [chuckles].

00:15:19.471 --> 00:15:21.130
- All right, well, glucose is bad for you

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:22.860
but not for the reason
that you might think.

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:24.910
- So, we have a little
better understanding now

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:29.590
of how sirtuins are turned on by fasting.

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:32.240
We talked about two other classes

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:34.610
of longevity genes in the first episode,

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:37.320
mTOR and AMPK,

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:38.470
do you want to go really briefly

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:41.110
through how these genes are impacted

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:44.640
when we restrict calories, when we fast?

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:45.880
- The first one is mTOR,

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:48.380
which stands for mammalian
target of rapamycin,

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:49.930
the drug rapamycin.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.100
We talked a little bit about
it in the last episode,

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:54.780
but let's refresh everybody's memory.

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:56.700
This is protein complex in the cell

00:15:56.700 --> 00:15:58.400
that registers amino acids.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.580
When you have a lot of
protein, you eat a big steak.

00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:04.660
It's going to activate
this mTOR protein complex

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:07.760
that allows the cell, causes
the cell to build things.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:09.440
It's one of the reasons
eating steak allows you

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:13.290
to build more muscle, but that's
not a recipe for longevity.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:15.500
What we know from many animal
studies, even in yeast,

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.360
if you downregulate the activity

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:21.440
of this mTOR protein
complex, you get longer life.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:22.273
Why?

00:16:22.273 --> 00:16:24.700
Because it's activating a
process called autophagy,

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:26.410
which recycles proteins.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:27.910
So when you're hungry,

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:30.580
this autophagy will get
all the old proteins,

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:32.780
put them in the recycling bin

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:34.830
and then bring them out as fresh proteins.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.240
And that seems to be really
important for longevity.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:39.270
In fact, even if you just inhibit mTOR

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.520
and stimulate autophagy, that's sufficient

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:43.610
to extend the lifespan of flies

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:46.800
and even mice by dramatic
amounts, even 30%.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:48.760
- So, [indistinct] mTOR,
we start with fasting,

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:50.800
we get longevity and sirtuins.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:53.893
We start with fascinating,
we get longevity in AMPK,

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:56.120
- Same thing.
- Same thing.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.210
- But in this-
- But in a different process.

00:16:58.210 --> 00:16:59.500
- Exactly, it's doing something else.

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:00.560
Right.
- And in this case,

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:03.400
when you're hungry, AMPK will go up.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:04.233
AMPK, if you're wondering,

00:17:04.233 --> 00:17:06.590
it stands for AMP-activated kinase,

00:17:06.590 --> 00:17:08.860
and that's just an enzyme
that responds to low energy.

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:11.120
So when you're hungry,
you'll make more of it.

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:12.640
And one of the main things that it does is

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:14.520
it makes more mitochondria.

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:16.580
We lose mitochondria as we get older.

00:17:16.580 --> 00:17:19.120
And when we exercise, we get more,

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:21.810
and this is a way of
artificially stimulating

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:22.960
that production.

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:24.640
Why do we need more mitochondria?

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:27.220
Well, they're important
for metabolizing things

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:29.310
that you eat but one of the main things

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:32.420
that they are used for is to
make energy, chemical energy.

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:35.220
So, when you activate
AMPK, you'll feel better.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.497
You'll have more energy
and you also fight aging.

00:17:37.497 --> 00:17:40.390
- And you're using the
words "you'll" a lot here.

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:41.490
You're talking about like,

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:43.850
presumably the people
watching and listening

00:17:43.850 --> 00:17:45.830
to this are human beings,

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:48.533
but we should note that,

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:53.573
again, much of this research
got done in model organisms.

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:57.053
Human studies are a lot tougher.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:59.030
- But we have them for humans.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:00.100
- We do have them for human,

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:00.933
but there are fewer of them.

00:18:00.933 --> 00:18:02.280
And there's like,

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:05.660
most of the sample sizes are much smaller.

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:07.170
- Yeah, but there's enough known about

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:11.010
both mTOR and MPK to make
pretty strong conclusions

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:13.460
that these are beneficial
to human health as well.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:14.938
- For example-
- But this is,

00:18:14.938 --> 00:18:16.150
I guess what I'm getting at here though,

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:18.930
is the reason why, I think a lot of people

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:20.900
that would hear,

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:23.150
well, there's all these animals studies,

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:25.580
we're making all these
conclusions on animal studies

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:28.230
and you don't have that
many human studies,

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:29.913
but human studies are,

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:32.270
they take a really long time.

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:34.140
They're really expensive.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:37.310
- Well, sure, and until
this is a 100% proven,

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:38.300
many of us will be dead.

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:41.900
So the point is, we're going
to give people the information

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:43.180
in this podcast, in the notes,

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:44.340
we're going to provide references.

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:46.870
They can read more to
make informed decisions

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:48.700
about whether they're
going to try certain diets,

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:51.760
lifestyles and even
medicines and supplements.

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:56.760
- So, what we know from
studies of mTOR and AMPK,

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:59.760
sirtuins is, tells our
cells that times are tough.

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.100
This triggers this metabolic shift

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:05.003
into a different form of energy,

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:09.200
and all of that, not all of that,

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:11.393
but much of that we
know from animal studies

00:19:11.393 --> 00:19:12.630
that we mentioned before.

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:14.720
The majority of the research

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:19.370
on lifespan extension
comes from model organisms,

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:21.030
comes from animals that aren't us.

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:24.380
But we're not just entering,

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:25.500
we're in a world right now,

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:29.490
where we're doing this research
on human beings as well.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:30.950
- We are, it's a fascinating time

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:32.500
because we've got two worlds

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:34.790
and I walk in both worlds.

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:37.190
One is, there's clinical
studies being done,

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:40.090
some of which I'm involved in
with NAD boosters at Harvard,

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:42.480
we've been testing two
years of those in humans,

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.110
placebo controlled, double blind trial.

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:46.230
- Like the gold standard stuff.

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:49.000
- Right, and those go to
scientific publications.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.200
But, because people
can read the literature

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:52.530
and hear about things,

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:55.080
there's this self
experimentation world as well.

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:57.232
A lot of it on the west coast of the USA

00:19:57.232 --> 00:19:59.080
[Matthew laughs]
and you hear stuff,

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:01.640
and these are anecdotal but
they also are interesting

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.130
anecdotes that guide the clinical trials.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:07.860
And it's this very interesting
time where we're in,

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:09.880
where there are parts of the population,

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:12.800
let's call it 1% of the
US that is not waiting

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:14.550
for the proof that this works.

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:17.190
But I do want to say before we move on

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:18.870
that there is pretty good evidence

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:22.450
that modulating these defenses in the body

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:24.310
in humans also works

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:25.900
and should extend longevity.

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:27.670
We're not just blind [stammers],

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:31.500
I'm not crazy experimenting
on myself and my father.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:33.940
What we know, actually is that drugs

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:37.100
that inhibit mTOR, rapamycin

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:40.440
in low dose, intermittent
does mimic fasting

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:44.160
and does boost immunity and
does give biochemical changes

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:48.190
that mimic fasting and predict longevity.

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:50.410
And there are people that
are taking rapamycin.

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:52.770
Again, we'll talk about
that in episode five.

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:55.280
But there's also metformin,

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:58.770
which is a diabetes drug
and that activates AMPK.

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:01.210
And that's also by looking at
tens of thousands of people,

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:03.220
who take metformin for type two diabetes,

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.820
been shown to slow down the occurrence,

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:08.950
not just of diabetes, but
other diseases of aging.

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:11.420
Together, just those facts

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:14.740
that I've told you make me convinced

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:17.210
that fasting and the drugs

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:19.630
that mimic fasting are
going to be important

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:24.280
for long-term health but also
wellness in your body today.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:25.560
- There's this other way of knowing,

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.800
this other form of evidence for this

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:28.870
that we haven't talked about yet,

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:31.469
that I think is important
to bring up here.

00:21:31.469 --> 00:21:34.560
There's the double blind,
placebo controlled studies

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.430
that are ongoing right
now that are showing

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:39.600
an impact in fasting on longevity.

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:42.100
There's like you say, experimental,

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:43.300
really interesting, like, wow,

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:44.890
that guy is looking really good

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:46.790
and he's been fasting,
sort of thing, right?

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:48.940
- Yeah.
- But then there's these,

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:51.980
large populations and subgroups

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:53.980
of populations, all across the world

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:57.220
who have fasted as a part
of tradition and culture

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:00.423
for hundreds, thousands of years.

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:05.220
And there's pretty good evidence
from these groups as well.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.197
- Right, well, let's name some of them.

00:22:07.197 --> 00:22:09.180
- The genes in India.
- In India.

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:12.807
They're probably the
most well-studied group.

00:22:12.807 --> 00:22:14.150
And there's scientific evidence

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:16.830
that they have the most
number of people over 70

00:22:16.830 --> 00:22:18.710
in good health than any other religion.

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:20.442
- Than any other group in India.

00:22:20.442 --> 00:22:21.275
- Fair enough.

00:22:21.275 --> 00:22:22.108
- And a lot of people [indistinct] go,

00:22:22.108 --> 00:22:24.970
oh, well, the genes are
often are also vegetarian,

00:22:24.970 --> 00:22:27.440
but lots of people in India are vegetarian

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:29.620
and the genes are even
doing better than them.

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:31.940
- Yes, and we'll get
to the vegetarian diet

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:33.210
and what the science says about that,

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:34.920
versus a meat based diet.

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:36.050
But there are other religions of,

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:39.620
Christians fast, there's
the Ramadan for Muslims.

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:41.540
These are not just coincidences,

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:43.050
these aren't just religious practices.

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:45.450
It's clear that humanity has figured out

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:46.560
that you feel better.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:49.680
You look better, you ultimately
are disease resistant.

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:51.760
It might even help cure diseases by going

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:53.890
through these periods of being hungry

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:56.670
or at least not having food in your tummy.

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:57.660
Why?

00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:01.020
Because it activates these
three longevity defenses

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:02.700
that we just mentioned.

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:04.840
- Now, let's get into
these really interesting,

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:06.573
sort of gold standard studies.

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:10.820
There is one out of the
Baylor College of Medicine.

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:12.170
It was run by [indistinct].

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:17.120
This was published just last year.

00:23:17.120 --> 00:23:20.950
And it showed that fasting
from Dawn to sunset

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:24.600
for four weeks improved
blood pressure, reduced BMI,

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:27.300
decreased weight circumference and,

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:28.920
and this is the important part.

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:29.960
I mean, it's all important,

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:33.840
but it upregulated DNA repair proteins.

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:38.030
This is what we saw on all
these model organisms as well.

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:39.810
- Yeah, there are dozens
of studies showing

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:42.870
that periods of fasting is beneficial

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:44.323
to people who are obese,

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:46.420
not just because they lose weight,

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:48.010
but they turn on their body's defenses.

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:49.680
They become more insulin sensitive.

00:23:49.680 --> 00:23:51.700
And their glucose levels come down.

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:53.330
Also showing that people
of regular weight,

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:55.053
like me, can benefit from fasting,

00:23:55.053 --> 00:23:57.440
there's a number of studies there.

00:23:57.440 --> 00:23:58.500
And these were all in the show notes.

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:00.970
If somebody wants to
go and check them out,

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:02.290
but what's really interesting is

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:05.840
that certain diseases, type one diabetes,

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:08.270
multiple sclerosis even cancer,

00:24:08.270 --> 00:24:11.440
those diseases seem to
also benefit from fasting,

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:15.050
including when you combine
chemotherapy with fasting,

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:17.720
you get this double benefit
for many types of cancers.

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:19.480
And then the final point that
I think is really interesting

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:22.300
is you can mimic fasting, say metformin,

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:24.570
a number of doctors prescribe metformin

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:26.720
to patients with prostate cancer,

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:30.940
because more studies are
showing that these mimicry,

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:33.130
these molecules that
mimic fasting or fasting

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:36.870
or combinations of both
will help you beat cancer.

00:24:36.870 --> 00:24:39.070
- And to you, when you see a bunch

00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:42.373
of different disease
states being affected.

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:44.930
You don't think that what's happening is

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:48.140
that each of these individual
diseases is being played upon.

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:49.670
You think that the thing upstream,

00:24:49.670 --> 00:24:52.500
the aging is being affected, right?

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.300
- Yeah, well, most specifically
those three defense

00:24:55.300 --> 00:24:56.830
components in the cell,

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:59.340
they take care of the
body, not just for aging

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:02.540
but to fight diseases in
young people, middle-aged,

00:25:02.540 --> 00:25:05.720
genetic diseases, even something
you might not think of,

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:07.330
like macular degeneration has been shown

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:11.560
to be slowed down and
even reversed by fasting.

00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:14.830
- Okay, so far we've been
