We've got different genes.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:35.590
Jim Nelson's work's been
informative in this.

00:34:35.590 --> 00:34:37.580
- Yeah, so Jim did a
important study in mice,

00:34:37.580 --> 00:34:38.460
again, not humans,

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:41.450
but it tells us that genetics is important

00:34:41.450 --> 00:34:44.680
'cause he took very similar mouse strains,

00:34:44.680 --> 00:34:47.350
strains of, some are called black 6

00:34:47.350 --> 00:34:48.630
and then some white ones.

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:51.960
And he crossed them together
to make of genetic diversity,

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:54.410
a colony of about a hundred
different types of mice

00:34:54.410 --> 00:34:57.440
and put them on the standard
caloric restriction protocol,

00:34:57.440 --> 00:34:58.860
which by recollection,

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:01.710
it was close to 35% of
what a mouse would eat,

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:04.360
given food all the time,
ad libitum, we call it.

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:07.003
And about half the strains
of mice lived longer.

00:35:07.950 --> 00:35:09.200
And then about a third

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:11.370
of what the remaining ones lived shorter.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:12.430
And that was a shock because we

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:14.260
