about doing an experiment

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:03.670
where we would have a
conversation around a table

00:43:03.670 --> 00:43:05.850
with people and then have the servers come

00:43:05.850 --> 00:43:07.380
with like, a birthday cake or something

00:43:07.380 --> 00:43:09.410
and you just like, watch them.

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:11.290
- We still should do that.

00:43:11.290 --> 00:43:13.650
But yeah, so glucose is a bad one.

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:16.690
Something else to avoid
is super high protein

00:43:16.690 --> 00:43:19.320
because mTOR, it can be activated

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:21.480
but you don't want to
activate it all the time.

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:23.350
'Cause it's not going to
turn on the autophagy,

00:43:23.350 --> 00:43:24.777
the defenses to recycle proteins.

00:43:24.777 --> 00:43:27.390
- And this one is going
to a lot of people off.

00:43:27.390 --> 00:43:28.960
- Well, yeah, there's a lot of people

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:32.500
who believe that carnivore diets
are the best for longevity.

00:43:32.500 --> 00:43:37.500
- And for some people, a lot
of aminos are appropriate.

00:43:40.090 --> 00:43:41.930
- Well, certainly if you're an athlete

00:43:41.930 --> 00:43:44.680
or you want to bulk up,
there are short-term gains.

00:43:44.680 --> 00:43:45.950
You'll feel better if you eat meat,

00:43:45.950 --> 00:43:49.370
you'll obviously have the
protein to build up that muscle.

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:52.120
But we can go through the
evidence when you look

00:43:52.120 --> 00:43:55.810
at populations at what they
eat and how long they live,

00:43:55.810 --> 00:43:58.240
as well as the short-term
effects when you eat

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:02.790
a high protein, carnivorous,
red meat based diet,

00:44:02.790 --> 00:44:05.830
those changes will be
good in the short run,

00:44:05.830 --> 00:44:07.740
but long-term, there's no evidence,

00:44:07.740 --> 00:44:09.260
in fact, I would say
there's counter evidence

00:44:09.260 --> 00:44:13.110
to that being beneficial for
longevity, if that's your goal.

00:44:13.110 --> 00:44:15.580
- And that's because of
the inhibition of TOR

00:44:15.580 --> 00:44:17.950
- In large part, yes, the
sirtuins will also get switched

00:44:17.950 --> 00:44:19.350
off by high protein as well.

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:22.203
- So, aminos are important.

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:25.780
We have to have them, if
we don't have them, we die.

00:44:25.780 --> 00:44:29.990
But you can get aminos from
plants as well as from animals.

00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:32.270
- Yeah, it's funny when I say

00:44:32.270 --> 00:44:34.535
I've gone vegetarian
recently, which is a fact-

00:44:34.535 --> 00:44:35.440
- Where are you going to get your protein?

00:44:35.440 --> 00:44:36.451
- Yeah, where do you get protein from

00:44:36.451 --> 00:44:38.010
but what do you think plants are made of?

00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:39.300
It's also mostly protein.

00:44:39.300 --> 00:44:41.700
Now, they're not as bioavailable.

00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:44.090
So, you're getting like
two thirds the amount

00:44:44.090 --> 00:44:45.080
as you would from a steak-
- Your body has

00:44:45.080 --> 00:44:46.320
to work a little harder for it.

00:44:46.320 --> 00:44:48.260
- Great, I want my body to work harder.

00:44:48.260 --> 00:44:49.680
It's good for it, it burns energy,

00:44:49.680 --> 00:44:52.470
it's also activating these
defenses as we mentioned.

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:55.920
So I'm now trying out this,
a full vegetarian diet.

00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:58.760
I'm not yet vegan but that
actually probably works

00:44:58.760 --> 00:45:01.747
even better for longevity
as the science will tell.

00:45:01.747 --> 00:45:03.963
- How's it working for you, you feeling?

00:45:05.230 --> 00:45:06.080
Do you miss meat?

00:45:06.930 --> 00:45:09.000
- I do, I love meat.

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:10.860
I think meat tastes great,

00:45:10.860 --> 00:45:15.400
but I'm more and more
inclined to enjoy [indistinct]

00:45:15.400 --> 00:45:16.960
as well.
- So we're starting

00:45:16.960 --> 00:45:19.190
to go down and talk about vegetarian diet,

00:45:19.190 --> 00:45:20.360
plant-based diets here.

00:45:20.360 --> 00:45:22.890
We're going to talk about a
lot of those different choices

00:45:22.890 --> 00:45:24.710
in a minute here, but I
do want to take us back

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:25.850
for just a second

00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:29.120
to what's happening.

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:30.690
Like, really was actually happening

00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:31.523
in the inside of our bodies.

00:45:31.523 --> 00:45:34.540
When [stammers] and I still eat meat,

00:45:34.540 --> 00:45:36.550
I haven't followed you down this path yet.

00:45:36.550 --> 00:45:37.573
I love the steak.

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:42.240
When I put a steak on
my plate and I eat it,

00:45:42.240 --> 00:45:44.653
what's happening, what
are those aminos doing?

00:45:45.820 --> 00:45:48.020
- Well, before you get aminos out of meat,

00:45:48.020 --> 00:45:50.310
you need to digest it
and so the first step is,

00:45:50.310 --> 00:45:52.260
acid in your stomach
is going to break down

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:54.270
that meat into amino acids,

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:57.670
and then your microbiomes
can utilize a lot of them.

00:45:57.670 --> 00:45:59.830
And then those amino acids
are also going to leach

00:45:59.830 --> 00:46:00.880
into your bloodstream.

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:03.970
So, now you've got these amino
acids circling in your body,

00:46:03.970 --> 00:46:06.290
circulating, and there are three ones

00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:08.520
that are particularly
important to know about,

00:46:08.520 --> 00:46:11.560
it's leucine, ISO leucine and valine

00:46:11.560 --> 00:46:14.570
also known as the
branched-chain amino acids.

00:46:14.570 --> 00:46:18.200
And these are used by the
body to sense protein intake.

00:46:18.200 --> 00:46:21.070
And the sensor is this
protein complex we talked

00:46:21.070 --> 00:46:23.150
about called mTOR.

00:46:23.150 --> 00:46:24.470
And when there's lots of these three

00:46:24.470 --> 00:46:27.490
branched-chain amino acids,
particularly leucine,

00:46:27.490 --> 00:46:28.460
it will be activated.

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:30.590
And this mTOR, the role is to say,

00:46:30.590 --> 00:46:32.450
wow, I got lots of amino acids,

00:46:32.450 --> 00:46:34.590
let's build muscle, let's repair cells,

00:46:34.590 --> 00:46:35.670
let's do all good stuff,

00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:37.860
which is why you you feel
good if you eat red meat

00:46:37.860 --> 00:46:40.780
and your microbiome can handle it.

00:46:40.780 --> 00:46:43.610
- Yeah, I feel great, I feel
like I can fight a lion.

00:46:43.610 --> 00:46:46.520
- Yeah, and you might have
to fight a lion to get it.

00:46:46.520 --> 00:46:50.330
But what actually is going
on that's deleterious

00:46:50.330 --> 00:46:54.530
in the long run is that mTOR
can do something even better

00:46:54.530 --> 00:46:56.760
for your longevity than build new muscle.

00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:59.110
It actually can turn on
this recycling program

00:46:59.110 --> 00:47:00.233
called autophagy.

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:03.030
And that is very clear.

00:47:03.030 --> 00:47:05.440
There's no debate as to
whether autophagy is good

00:47:05.440 --> 00:47:06.640
for you or bad for you.

00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:08.120
It's definitely good.

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:11.090
So, what I think people could do is to,

00:47:11.090 --> 00:47:12.800
if they want to eat meat, go for it,

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:17.760
but try to focus on
plant-based food more often

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:20.620
so that there are periods during
the week when there aren't

00:47:20.620 --> 00:47:22.920
as many, leucine, isoleucine,

00:47:22.920 --> 00:47:25.020
valine molecules floating
around in your body.

00:47:25.020 --> 00:47:27.870
So that you have a chance
for your mTOR downregulation

00:47:27.870 --> 00:47:29.310
to recycle proteins.

00:47:29.310 --> 00:47:30.640
Now, why do you want to recycle proteins?

00:47:30.640 --> 00:47:33.220
You might say, well, who cares
about recycling proteins.

00:47:33.220 --> 00:47:35.760
Well, Alzheimer's disease is
a good example of proteins

00:47:35.760 --> 00:47:39.050
that get modified and
accumulate in your brain.

00:47:39.050 --> 00:47:41.130
And it happens not just in
the brain, but in all tissues.

00:47:41.130 --> 00:47:43.230
We have these old proteins that linger,

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:45.270
'cause us to get old to malfunction,

00:47:45.270 --> 00:47:47.390
but it's reversible and it's reversible,

00:47:47.390 --> 00:47:50.410
by fasting, it's reversible
also by having lower levels

00:47:50.410 --> 00:47:51.880
of these branched-chain amino acids,

00:47:51.880 --> 00:47:53.360
at least part of the week.

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:58.360
- Let's talk about these
primarily plant-based diets.

00:47:58.570 --> 00:48:01.070
There's lots, I mean, there
is vegan diets out there.

00:48:01.070 --> 00:48:01.903
You said like,

00:48:03.164 --> 00:48:05.750
for many people, that's probably
going to be the gold standard

00:48:05.750 --> 00:48:07.090
but a lot of these plant-based diets

00:48:07.090 --> 00:48:08.310
have a little bit of meat in them.

00:48:08.310 --> 00:48:10.690
There's the Mediterranean diet.

00:48:10.690 --> 00:48:12.760
There's the Okinawan diet.

00:48:12.760 --> 00:48:13.740
- Lacto-ovo vegetarianism.

00:48:13.740 --> 00:48:16.680
- Right, lacto-ovo vegetarian diet.

00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:18.860
Is there anything you
want to say about these

00:48:18.860 --> 00:48:21.300
or can we talk about them as

00:48:21.300 --> 00:48:26.090
an umbrella group, don't you
call 'em like blue zone diets?

00:48:26.090 --> 00:48:28.040
- Yeah, well, let's talk
about blue zones in a minute.

00:48:28.040 --> 00:48:29.880
I think, what's interesting is that,

00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:31.550
there was a really big study

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:33.690
by the Adventist Health Group,

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:35.070
2013.
- Yeah.

00:48:35.070 --> 00:48:35.903
- Let's talk about that,

00:48:35.903 --> 00:48:37.930
because what they
calculated was the chance

00:48:37.930 --> 00:48:41.270
of dying based on various diets

00:48:41.270 --> 00:48:43.350
and these were thousands of people.

00:48:43.350 --> 00:48:44.940
And what they found was that,

00:48:44.940 --> 00:48:46.860
what's called the hazard ratio,

00:48:46.860 --> 00:48:49.580
went down the more vegetarian
and vegan you were.

00:48:49.580 --> 00:48:50.413
What does that mean?

00:48:50.413 --> 00:48:51.930
Your chance of dying goes down.

00:48:51.930 --> 00:48:54.060
And the number goes from zero to one.

00:48:54.060 --> 00:48:57.490
Whereas, one is you're pretty
likely to die tomorrow,

00:48:57.490 --> 00:49:00.170
whereas a low number, which is around 80,

00:49:00.170 --> 00:49:04.500
means you've got 20% less
chance of dying on any given day

00:49:04.500 --> 00:49:06.010
with this hazard ratio.

00:49:06.010 --> 00:49:07.710
So, the numbers are the following,

00:49:08.720 --> 00:49:12.410
non-vegetarians are at
one, if you call that one,

00:49:12.410 --> 00:49:17.090
compared to that, the next
best one was semi vegetarians

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:18.540
at 0.92

00:49:18.540 --> 00:49:22.380
and lacto-ovo vegetarians, 0.91.

00:49:22.380 --> 00:49:27.380
Okay, so almost a 10%
reduction in mortality, death.

00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:30.150
And then you get into vegans,

00:49:30.150 --> 00:49:34.170
with 0.85, so, that's
15% reduction in death,

00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:37.140
and then the best one
was Pesco vegetarian.

00:49:37.140 --> 00:49:37.995
So, getting a little bit of meat-

00:49:37.995 --> 00:49:39.540
- Little bit of fish,
- Yeah, little bit of meat

00:49:39.540 --> 00:49:42.410
from fish, probably the fish
oils in there are beneficial.

00:49:42.410 --> 00:49:44.260
And then you're down to 0.81.

00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:47.100
So that's a 19% reduction

00:49:47.100 --> 00:49:49.460
in your chance of death at
any given day, late in life.

00:49:49.460 --> 00:49:51.770
- Okay, now, that's all cause mortality,

00:49:51.770 --> 00:49:55.510
but we can also now see
this in biologic aging

00:49:55.510 --> 00:49:58.370
because we now have clocks that we can use

00:49:58.370 --> 00:50:02.370
to measure our biological age.

00:50:02.370 --> 00:50:04.400
And there are studies now also

00:50:04.400 --> 00:50:05.750
that are showing that these diets

00:50:05.750 --> 00:50:10.750
are effectively reducing
biological aging as well.

00:50:10.990 --> 00:50:12.170
- Right, and there were a couple of these,

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:13.400
there's a one-year study looking

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:16.660
at a diet of mostly vegetarian.

00:50:16.660 --> 00:50:18.800
But the one that I think
is really exciting is one

00:50:18.800 --> 00:50:20.960
that just came out a couple of months ago

00:50:20.960 --> 00:50:23.830
that looked at mostly women

00:50:23.830 --> 00:50:26.820
on a Mediterranean diet plus
exercise over two years.

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:29.040
And they could now calculate what happens

00:50:29.040 --> 00:50:31.300
to your biological age
over those two years,

00:50:31.300 --> 00:50:32.680
having switched the diet

00:50:32.680 --> 00:50:34.290
and they had various recommendations.

00:50:34.290 --> 00:50:37.750
It's very typical
Mediterranean, limit alcohol,

00:50:37.750 --> 00:50:39.650
if you have red wine, that's fine.

00:50:39.650 --> 00:50:43.550
A little bit of fish, only
use olive oil as your fat

00:50:43.550 --> 00:50:45.212
and focus mostly on plants.

00:50:45.212 --> 00:50:46.400
That was their lifestyle.

00:50:46.400 --> 00:50:50.050
And then exercise was walking
and some other weightlifting.

00:50:50.050 --> 00:50:51.700
- That's the Fourier study.

00:50:51.700 --> 00:50:52.533
- Yeah.
- Okay,

00:50:52.533 --> 00:50:53.970
so the interesting thing about this study,

00:50:53.970 --> 00:50:55.950
at least to me, the thing
that jumped out at me is

00:50:55.950 --> 00:50:59.250
it came out of Geovanna Masala's lab

00:50:59.250 --> 00:51:02.080
at the Cancer Institute in Italy.

00:51:02.080 --> 00:51:05.600
And like, [mumbles] well, why do people

00:51:05.600 --> 00:51:07.250
whose primary responsibility is

00:51:07.250 --> 00:51:09.310
to study cancer care about aging?

00:51:09.310 --> 00:51:10.430
And it comes back to this thing

00:51:10.430 --> 00:51:11.480
that you've been saying all along,

00:51:11.480 --> 00:51:14.420
which is that if we can reduce
people's biological aging,

00:51:14.420 --> 00:51:18.640
we can cut diseases off
where they actually start.

00:51:18.640 --> 00:51:20.040
- Well, that's exactly right.

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:22.230
These diseases that we
think are just diseases

00:51:22.230 --> 00:51:24.570
are mostly caused by aging itself.

00:51:24.570 --> 00:51:27.350
And that slowing down
that clock by fasting

00:51:27.350 --> 00:51:29.810
or eating the right
foods, Mediterranean diet,

00:51:29.810 --> 00:51:31.020
is now shown not just

00:51:31.020 --> 00:51:33.120
to slow down the ticking of the clock,

00:51:33.120 --> 00:51:35.080
but probably reverses your age as well.

00:51:35.080 --> 00:51:37.680
And that's a mind blowing concept.

00:51:37.680 --> 00:51:39.070
You have to think about that for a minute,

00:51:39.070 --> 00:51:41.340
that it's possible by changing your diet

00:51:42.400 --> 00:51:45.170
to reverse your biological age.

00:51:45.170 --> 00:51:49.820
- One of the diets that
stands to do this well,

00:51:49.820 --> 00:51:52.300
likely, that I know you're a big fan of

00:51:52.300 --> 00:51:53.873
is the Okinawan diet.

00:51:55.050 --> 00:51:57.690
You were on the Okinawan diet
for quite some time, right?

00:51:57.690 --> 00:52:00.290
- Yeah, I was, the Wilcox
brothers wrote a book

00:52:00.290 --> 00:52:02.800
in the 2000s that I loved.

00:52:02.800 --> 00:52:05.170
And it's mostly carbohydrate,

00:52:05.170 --> 00:52:06.380
so there's a fair amount of rice

00:52:06.380 --> 00:52:07.960
but probably could have done better

00:52:07.960 --> 00:52:09.990
with a bit of brown rice,
white rice sends your glucose

00:52:09.990 --> 00:52:10.850
through the roof.
- Right.

00:52:10.850 --> 00:52:13.550
- But mostly what I
was eating were Chinese

00:52:13.550 --> 00:52:15.200
and or Japanese vegetables
that I could get

00:52:15.200 --> 00:52:19.430
at the local market, so
they're organic, fresh,

00:52:19.430 --> 00:52:21.940
green, full of vitamins and soy.

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:24.940
Mostly it was that, it was a
plant-based, soy-based diet

00:52:24.940 --> 00:52:26.530
with a little bit of fish.

00:52:26.530 --> 00:52:28.570
And I looked great, I felt great.

00:52:28.570 --> 00:52:30.340
And then my kids came along

00:52:30.340 --> 00:52:32.170
and we ate pizza for a few years,

00:52:32.170 --> 00:52:35.150
but it was great while it lasted.

00:52:35.150 --> 00:52:38.850
And if you look at the Okinawans,
on the island of Japan,

00:52:38.850 --> 00:52:42.280
they are arguably the longest
lived people on the planet,

00:52:42.280 --> 00:52:43.760
but they have other things
that are good for them.

00:52:43.760 --> 00:52:45.470
Things like, social
structure and exercise,

00:52:45.470 --> 00:52:46.763
they work until they're
in their 90s or 100s-

00:52:46.763 --> 00:52:48.840
- None of these things happen in a void.

00:52:48.840 --> 00:52:51.910
- Right, but that lifestyle
is probably the ultimate

00:52:51.910 --> 00:52:53.170
for human longevity.

00:52:53.170 --> 00:52:55.730
And we know this because
if Okinawans moved to say,

00:52:55.730 --> 00:52:58.170
Hawaii, which a lot of Japanese did.

00:52:58.170 --> 00:53:00.090
Then they don't live long anymore.

00:53:00.090 --> 00:53:00.923
- Yeah.

00:53:01.840 --> 00:53:04.440
You talked about lifestyle,
this is a really big part of,

00:53:04.440 --> 00:53:08.010
you said you wanted to get
back to the blue zones idea.

00:53:08.010 --> 00:53:09.900
You go across the world,

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:13.530
you look at these communities
of long lived people.

00:53:13.530 --> 00:53:16.993
They have a lot of things in common,

00:53:18.630 --> 00:53:20.750
and we've been talking about diet today.

00:53:20.750 --> 00:53:22.985
So, let's just jump into that.

00:53:22.985 --> 00:53:25.360
And there has been
criticism of blue zones too.

00:53:25.360 --> 00:53:27.030
- Yeah, so Dr. Harriet hall has said

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:28.280
that those demographic data,

00:53:28.280 --> 00:53:30.330
people's ages weren't actually correct.

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:32.890
But if you dig into it,
they did a really good job.

00:53:32.890 --> 00:53:35.320
So, Dan Buettner I
think is right in saying

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:37.640
that these people tend
to live a long time,

00:53:37.640 --> 00:53:40.450
out of hundreds of people that
they check the records of,

00:53:40.450 --> 00:53:42.900
going back in the city records,

00:53:42.900 --> 00:53:45.070
there was one person that was misjudged.

00:53:45.070 --> 00:53:46.590
What was it?

00:53:46.590 --> 00:53:47.700
- They thought they were like, 111

00:53:47.700 --> 00:53:49.810
and they were actually 107 or something.

00:53:49.810 --> 00:53:50.660
- Yeah, big deal.

00:53:51.670 --> 00:53:53.650
- Of course, all studies have caveats,

00:53:53.650 --> 00:53:56.570
but this I think is really
clear that areas of the world

00:53:56.570 --> 00:53:59.970
that have the right
combination of foods, exercise,

00:53:59.970 --> 00:54:02.830
social structure, they do live longer.

00:54:02.830 --> 00:54:05.890
This is no surprise, even in yeast when

00:54:05.890 --> 00:54:07.090
we don't give them social life

00:54:07.090 --> 00:54:09.990
but when we modify their
diet or give them food

00:54:09.990 --> 00:54:13.090
in a time restricted fashion,
they also live longer.

00:54:13.090 --> 00:54:16.440
So, I don't understand,
why it's controversial.

00:54:16.440 --> 00:54:19.120
- There's one more idea

00:54:19.970 --> 00:54:22.130
about how we should eat

00:54:22.130 --> 00:54:24.760
that I know you're a big fan of,

00:54:24.760 --> 00:54:27.690
there's less research behind it,

00:54:27.690 --> 00:54:32.210
but you believe pretty strongly I'd say

00:54:32.210 --> 00:54:35.690
and there is evidence, but
I think it's still emerging

00:54:35.690 --> 00:54:37.310
and there needs to be more research.

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:39.910
But, this idea of xenohormesis,

00:54:39.910 --> 00:54:42.140
this idea of eating plants that have,

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:43.780
not just eating a plant-based diet,

00:54:43.780 --> 00:54:46.023
but specifically focusing on plants

00:54:46.023 --> 00:54:48.853
that have experienced stress.

00:54:50.170 --> 00:54:54.003
Talk about that and why you are,

00:54:57.273 --> 00:54:59.433
so focused on this idea.

00:55:00.760 --> 00:55:03.120
- Well, the xenohormesis concept,

00:55:03.120 --> 00:55:04.900
Konrad Howitz and I, coined this term

00:55:04.900 --> 00:55:06.420
in the mid 2000s,

00:55:06.420 --> 00:55:08.150
trying to explain why

00:55:08.150 --> 00:55:10.370
so many plant molecules are good for us?

00:55:10.370 --> 00:55:11.810
It just cannot be a coincidence.

00:55:11.810 --> 00:55:13.830
And we came up with this idea,

00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:16.900
really prompted by a 2003 nature paper

00:55:16.900 --> 00:55:19.610
that we co published,
that found that there were

00:55:19.610 --> 00:55:23.490
at least 20 plant molecules
called polyphenols

00:55:23.490 --> 00:55:26.960
that activate the sirtuin
enzyme called Sirt1,

00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:29.850
the number one out of
the seven family members.

00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:31.010
And when I looked into it,

00:55:31.010 --> 00:55:34.040
these polyphenols do
remarkable things to the body.

00:55:34.040 --> 00:55:35.950
The one that got the most media attention,

00:55:35.950 --> 00:55:38.740
because it's in red wine is resveratrol,

00:55:38.740 --> 00:55:42.540
but there's piceatannol and
there's fisetin and quercetin

00:55:42.540 --> 00:55:44.490
these are supplements that
people are getting excited

00:55:44.490 --> 00:55:47.020
about only now, but when you look into it,

00:55:47.020 --> 00:55:51.290
they activate and inhibit
pathways or proteins in the body

00:55:51.290 --> 00:55:53.920
that are known to be important
for health and longevity.

00:55:53.920 --> 00:55:56.220
And in some cases, the same molecule

00:55:56.220 --> 00:55:58.810
will inhibit one protein
and activate another one

00:55:58.810 --> 00:56:01.520
in just the right way to promote health.

00:56:01.520 --> 00:56:02.860
That cannot be a coincidence.

00:56:02.860 --> 00:56:06.570
There's no way that that
could just happen randomly.

00:56:06.570 --> 00:56:09.910
So the idea is that
we've evolved mechanisms

00:56:09.910 --> 00:56:11.620
to sense when our food supply,

00:56:11.620 --> 00:56:13.280
the plants that we eat are stressed.

00:56:13.280 --> 00:56:15.190
- Because it's like an
early warning system.

00:56:15.190 --> 00:56:16.933
- Right, we're humans, we can see

00:56:16.933 --> 00:56:18.190
that the plants are wilting

00:56:18.190 --> 00:56:20.800
and we know that the crops are dehydrated.

00:56:20.800 --> 00:56:22.340
We can measure the soil.

00:56:22.340 --> 00:56:25.290
Most organisms on the
planet don't even have eyes.

00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:28.890
How would they know if their
food supply is going to run out

00:56:28.890 --> 00:56:33.680
unless they sensed the stress,
the fear in those foodstuffs.

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:36.030
And so when you stress a plant,
you get more resveratrol,

00:56:36.030 --> 00:56:38.570
you get more quercetin, piceatannol.

00:56:38.570 --> 00:56:40.579
And if you look at-
- What kind of plants?

00:56:40.579 --> 00:56:42.030
we should say, I mean,
it's not every plant

00:56:42.030 --> 00:56:43.230
that we're going to get those things-

00:56:43.230 --> 00:56:44.200
- Of course, it's every plant.

00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:45.490
- Is it every plant that-
- Well, plants have

00:56:45.490 --> 00:56:49.650
sirtuin genes and they need
to survive just like we do.

00:56:49.650 --> 00:56:51.690
And they make these polyphenols in order

00:56:51.690 --> 00:56:53.390
to survive when they run out of,

00:56:53.390 --> 00:56:56.100
or running out of water and nutrients-

00:56:56.100 --> 00:56:57.620
- Okay, where are we
going to get most, though?

00:56:57.620 --> 00:57:01.210
or even preyed upon by caterpillars,

00:57:01.210 --> 00:57:03.510
but in our food supply a lot

00:57:03.510 --> 00:57:07.220
of plants have very
few of these molecules.

00:57:07.220 --> 00:57:08.053
Why?

00:57:08.053 --> 00:57:10.290
Because we think that the faster they grow

00:57:10.290 --> 00:57:12.510
and that the less stress
that they have, the better.

00:57:12.510 --> 00:57:14.170
It's actually more profitable for a farmer

00:57:14.170 --> 00:57:16.640
to grow plants that grow really
quickly and have no stress,

00:57:16.640 --> 00:57:18.110
but are they better for us?

00:57:18.110 --> 00:57:19.850
Absolutely not, so how do we know

00:57:19.850 --> 00:57:20.930
if a food has been stressed?

00:57:20.930 --> 00:57:22.940
Well, you can start
with the generalization

00:57:22.940 --> 00:57:25.740
that if they're grown out
in a field organically,

00:57:25.740 --> 00:57:28.710
without pesticides, probably
this most stressed, right?

00:57:28.710 --> 00:57:31.120
But also there are foods that
are intentionally stressed.

00:57:31.120 --> 00:57:32.770
Red wine is one,

00:57:32.770 --> 00:57:37.770
the best red wines are ones
where the vines are dehydrated

00:57:37.840 --> 00:57:39.800
or have fungus growing on them.

00:57:39.800 --> 00:57:41.210
There's a good reason for that.

00:57:41.210 --> 00:57:43.570
We know that they taste better.

00:57:43.570 --> 00:57:44.403
Why?

00:57:44.403 --> 00:57:46.960
Because Xenohormetic molecules are made

00:57:46.960 --> 00:57:49.860
along with molecules that taste good

00:57:49.860 --> 00:57:53.530
and are filled with color,
it's the plant defenses.

00:57:53.530 --> 00:57:56.790
And so what I look for are
plants that are organic,

00:57:56.790 --> 00:57:59.700
local and colorful
because those are the ones

00:57:59.700 --> 00:58:02.110
that are most likely to
have those molecules.

00:58:02.110 --> 00:58:05.670
In the case of red wine,
choose grape varieties

00:58:05.670 --> 00:58:07.810
that are stress sensitive.

00:58:07.810 --> 00:58:09.780
Pinot noir is one of the most sensitive,

00:58:09.780 --> 00:58:12.080
if not the most sensitive, grape variety.

00:58:12.080 --> 00:58:14.220
And that's why it has the most resveratrol

00:58:14.220 --> 00:58:15.520
of any other type of wine.

00:58:16.680 --> 00:58:20.610
- Let's bring this all together now.

00:58:20.610 --> 00:58:23.510
There's a lot of information here,

00:58:23.510 --> 00:58:25.670
but there's sort of a checklist.

00:58:25.670 --> 00:58:27.200
And,

00:58:27.200 --> 00:58:28.310
so let's talk about like,

00:58:28.310 --> 00:58:30.930
if people are going to have
like four takeaways here,

00:58:30.930 --> 00:58:31.993
five takeaways.

00:58:34.000 --> 00:58:34.913
Eat less often.

00:58:36.710 --> 00:58:39.280
You would say, start
with eating less often

00:58:39.280 --> 00:58:42.780
by skipping one meal a
day and moving from there.

00:58:42.780 --> 00:58:44.157
- Yeah, and don't snack and don't snack.

00:58:44.157 --> 00:58:45.270
- And don't snack.
- Yep.

00:58:45.270 --> 00:58:47.660
Avoid sugary drinks and foods.

00:58:47.660 --> 00:58:50.880
- Okay, eat less often when
you do eat, kill the sugar.

00:58:50.880 --> 00:58:51.720
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:58:51.720 --> 00:58:56.430
- I focus on, I try to
eat artificial sweeteners

00:58:56.430 --> 00:58:59.550
and natural ones, so
Stevia is a big one for me

00:58:59.550 --> 00:59:02.220
and there are some others
that are out on the market

00:59:02.220 --> 00:59:04.640
that are naturally occurring sweeteners.

00:59:04.640 --> 00:59:09.640
- Okay, start working toward
reducing your meat intake.

00:59:09.930 --> 00:59:13.773
If you're dieting, if your
diet is aimed at longevity,

00:59:15.040 --> 00:59:16.330
very likely, you're going to need

00:59:16.330 --> 00:59:19.110
to drop your meat intake.

00:59:19.110 --> 00:59:21.320
- Yeah, it causes a lot of concern

00:59:21.320 --> 00:59:23.690
of people who are pro meat

00:59:23.690 --> 00:59:25.460
but the data is the data.

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:27.700
And we've got these
references in our show notes

00:59:27.700 --> 00:59:30.410
that reducing the amount of red meat

00:59:30.410 --> 00:59:32.860
and in particular processed meats,

00:59:32.860 --> 00:59:34.137
is beneficial for long-term health

00:59:34.137 --> 00:59:36.500
and even prevention of cancer.

00:59:36.500 --> 00:59:38.957
One of the things that's not
appreciated about red meat.

00:59:38.957 --> 00:59:42.530
And I'll just mention this is that

00:59:42.530 --> 00:59:44.440
it's not just that they have more

00:59:44.440 --> 00:59:46.300
of these three branched-chain amino acids

00:59:46.300 --> 00:59:50.270
that prevents mTOR from doing
its good stuff to the body.

00:59:50.270 --> 00:59:55.140
There's also a molecule
called TMA that is in the meat

00:59:55.140 --> 00:59:56.450
that goes into the bloodstream,

00:59:56.450 --> 01:00:00.130
the liver turns it into a
toxic molecule called T-M-A-O.

01:00:00.130 --> 01:00:03.790
And this TMAO is showing,
at least in animals,

01:00:03.790 --> 01:00:05.870
probably in people, to enhance,

01:00:05.870 --> 01:00:08.850
accelerate cardiovascular disease.

01:00:08.850 --> 01:00:10.780
So I would say-
- And cancer too, right?

01:00:10.780 --> 01:00:12.060
Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:00:12.060 --> 01:00:13.280
- And so, what we want to do is

01:00:13.280 --> 01:00:16.210
to limit the nitrates,
the preserved meats,

01:00:16.210 --> 01:00:19.470
if you like a good steak
by all means eat one,

01:00:19.470 --> 01:00:20.660
it's not going to kill you.

01:00:20.660 --> 01:00:22.950
But if you try to just push yourself

01:00:22.950 --> 01:00:26.740
towards a more plant-based,
plant focused diet

01:00:26.740 --> 01:00:28.970
and maybe have some red wine occasionally,

01:00:28.970 --> 01:00:31.560
a lot of olive oil with oleic acid,

01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:33.740
which activates sirtuins as well.

01:00:33.740 --> 01:00:37.360
The Mediterranean diet is
the one that I think is,

01:00:37.360 --> 01:00:40.400
likely to be the easiest
to do in the Western world

01:00:40.400 --> 01:00:42.580
and to have the biggest bang for the buck.

01:00:42.580 --> 01:00:45.320
You can go a little bit
further, like I have,

01:00:45.320 --> 01:00:48.370
don't eat meat and eventually
maybe I'll give up everything,

01:00:48.370 --> 01:00:49.980
that's dairy and eggs.

01:00:49.980 --> 01:00:52.870
But the Mediterranean diet
is a very enjoyable diet.

01:00:52.870 --> 01:00:55.710
I lived on it for over a decade.

01:00:55.710 --> 01:00:58.150
And one of the things
that's not well known is

01:00:58.150 --> 01:01:01.380
that there are studies
of Mediterranean diet

01:01:01.380 --> 01:01:03.320
versus Western diet.

01:01:03.320 --> 01:01:04.630
And it's a massive difference.

01:01:04.630 --> 01:01:09.630
There's one here that was
published in, just 2000.

01:01:09.920 --> 01:01:11.700
There was a reduction in mortality-

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:12.980
- In 2000 or?

01:01:12.980 --> 01:01:14.715
in 2000, the year 2000.

01:01:14.715 --> 01:01:17.064
- So, it's 20 Something years ago.

01:01:17.064 --> 01:01:18.530
- It's 20 something years ago.

01:01:18.530 --> 01:01:19.750
But there are a lot of studies since,

01:01:19.750 --> 01:01:22.630
there's at least five studies
that have backed this up.

01:01:22.630 --> 01:01:25.713
If you switch to a Mediterranean diet,

01:01:26.770 --> 01:01:29.050
if you're under 80 and you do that,

01:01:29.050 --> 01:01:31.280
you reduce your mortality,

01:01:31.280 --> 01:01:34.971
chances of death on any given day by 31%.

01:01:34.971 --> 01:01:36.430
- [mumbles] like, if you're under 80,

01:01:36.430 --> 01:01:38.490
which means you can still
take advantage of this,

01:01:38.490 --> 01:01:41.020
even if you're way deep into your life.

01:01:41.020 --> 01:01:43.580
- Right, and I would say,
probably in this study,

01:01:43.580 --> 01:01:46.250
they just didn't include
it, people over 80.

01:01:46.250 --> 01:01:47.083
So I don't know for a fact that

01:01:47.083 --> 01:01:48.680
it wouldn't work on 80 year olds,

01:01:48.680 --> 01:01:51.220
- Sure, right.
- But yeah, that's the point

01:01:51.220 --> 01:01:52.760
that's really important,

01:01:52.760 --> 01:01:54.850
often when I give talks to crowds,

01:01:54.850 --> 01:01:57.450
they are elderly.

01:01:57.450 --> 01:02:00.450
And often the question comes
up, is it too late for me?

01:02:00.450 --> 01:02:02.200
The answer is, it's never too late.

01:02:03.090 --> 01:02:07.540
Dietary changes in older people
can have massive benefits.

01:02:07.540 --> 01:02:10.423
Some people say, oh you need
to have a carry a bit of fat,

01:02:11.480 --> 01:02:13.700
if you're over 80, you should
have a little bit of body fat.

01:02:13.700 --> 01:02:16.660
- There was a study that a
lot of people latched on to

01:02:16.660 --> 01:02:17.570
that they got really excited about

01:02:17.570 --> 01:02:20.680
because it was like, oh, if
you're older, you can be fat.

01:02:20.680 --> 01:02:22.550
- Yeah, well, it turns
out that's not true.

01:02:22.550 --> 01:02:24.883
When those studies have been redone,

01:02:25.810 --> 01:02:29.430
being lean as an older
person is also beneficial.

01:02:29.430 --> 01:02:30.990
If you look at, go to a nursing home,

01:02:30.990 --> 01:02:32.440
I don't know how many
people listening have been

01:02:32.440 --> 01:02:35.040
to a nursing home but when you go there,

01:02:35.040 --> 01:02:37.220
look around who's alive.

01:02:37.220 --> 01:02:40.930
They're not giant men who are obese.

01:02:40.930 --> 01:02:42.763
They're little women who are skinny.

01:02:44.290 --> 01:02:45.930
There's an experiment right there.

01:02:45.930 --> 01:02:48.240
It's obvious, right in plain sight.

01:02:48.240 --> 01:02:49.683
- Okay, so,

01:02:50.810 --> 01:02:51.643
eat less.

01:02:52.900 --> 01:02:56.580
Start working toward
fasting, cut the sugar,

01:02:56.580 --> 01:02:59.270
cut the meat, eat the veggies.

01:02:59.270 --> 01:03:00.500
Don't cut the meat and respond

01:03:00.500 --> 01:03:02.893
with a bunch of carbohydrates,
eat the veggies.

01:03:04.460 --> 01:03:05.940
And if you are going to eat the veggies

01:03:05.940 --> 01:03:08.390
and you can find ones that
have been stressed out

01:03:09.520 --> 01:03:12.610
and are enjoying a little Xenohormetic,

01:03:12.610 --> 01:03:15.970
you may enjoy a little
Xenohormetic effect from that.

01:03:15.970 --> 01:03:17.710
- That's great and there's even an order,

01:03:17.710 --> 01:03:18.820
which you can eat your meals

01:03:18.820 --> 01:03:20.420
to reduce the blood sugar spike.

01:03:20.420 --> 01:03:24.400
You can put the sugar
at the end of the meal.

01:03:24.400 --> 01:03:26.000
So, dessert is fine if you,

01:03:26.000 --> 01:03:28.270
but put it at the end,
don't start with sugar

01:03:28.270 --> 01:03:29.470
and don't start with the carbohydrate

01:03:29.470 --> 01:03:31.650
because that will immediately
spike your glucose.

01:03:31.650 --> 01:03:32.483
- So, if you are
[mumbles] go back to that,

01:03:32.483 --> 01:03:34.140
like the second and third point,

01:03:34.140 --> 01:03:35.750
if you are going to eat sugar,

01:03:35.750 --> 01:03:37.800
eat it at the end of a meal.

01:03:37.800 --> 01:03:40.430
- Right, what you ate during the meal

01:03:40.430 --> 01:03:42.695
and in which order also
makes a difference.

01:03:42.695 --> 01:03:44.901
[overlapped chatter]

01:03:44.901 --> 01:03:46.210
- that's just,
- Oh my goodness,

01:03:46.210 --> 01:03:48.410
eating candy during the
day is just going to

01:03:48.410 --> 01:03:50.960
make you feel lethargic
when that sugar goes away.

01:03:50.960 --> 01:03:52.330
- Well, we're doing this
really horrible thing

01:03:52.330 --> 01:03:55.860
to children by telling
them that their lives are,

01:03:55.860 --> 01:03:57.460
like sugar is this really great thing

01:03:57.460 --> 01:03:58.640
that they should have all the time.

01:03:58.640 --> 01:04:00.160
They should be able to have
snacks that are ladened

01:04:00.160 --> 01:04:01.670
with sugar and [murmurs] breakfast

01:04:01.670 --> 01:04:02.600
that starts with sugar and-

01:04:02.600 --> 01:04:05.670
- Yeah, well, I blame the
food industry for that.

01:04:05.670 --> 01:04:07.270
'Cause they want to sell more food.

01:04:07.270 --> 01:04:11.050
And we've been taught that
children should never go hungry.

01:04:11.050 --> 01:04:12.700
Breakfast is the best meal of the day.

01:04:12.700 --> 01:04:17.200
And we've got an obesity
epidemic in kids, no surprise.

01:04:17.200 --> 01:04:18.670
And what parent doesn't feel guilty

01:04:18.670 --> 01:04:22.630
when their kid feels hunger,
but for longterm health,

01:04:22.630 --> 01:04:24.740
even programming the epigenome

01:04:24.740 --> 01:04:29.740
for long term health requires
some hunger in individuals.

01:04:30.880 --> 01:04:33.670
And what we do to kids
is we say, big breakfast,

01:04:33.670 --> 01:04:35.430
here's a snack, here's your lunch,

01:04:35.430 --> 01:04:38.239
terrible lunch, typically
at schools, public schools,

01:04:38.239 --> 01:04:42.300
big dinner, go to bed full,
wake up, eat some more.

01:04:42.300 --> 01:04:46.460
We're not just causing problems
for these kids physiology

01:04:46.460 --> 01:04:48.770
for the long run, 20, 30 years later,

01:04:48.770 --> 01:04:50.690
'cause their epigenome is now set

01:04:50.690 --> 01:04:53.237
for feast not famine.
- Right.

01:04:53.237 --> 01:04:55.340
- But we're also setting
them up for obesity,

01:04:55.340 --> 01:04:59.860
which shuts off their
survival programs right now.

01:04:59.860 --> 01:05:02.200
So, their bodies are aging quicker

01:05:02.200 --> 01:05:03.400
than they otherwise would.

01:05:03.400 --> 01:05:08.400
- I wonder if that's not a
really important frame to put,

01:05:09.760 --> 01:05:12.780
what we call the obesity epidemic into is,

01:05:12.780 --> 01:05:15.380
it's not just an epidemic
of making people bigger.

01:05:15.380 --> 01:05:18.890
It's an epidemic of
making people age faster.

01:05:18.890 --> 01:05:21.680
- Absolutely and we're
going to see the impact

01:05:21.680 --> 01:05:23.890
of this in two or three decades from now

01:05:23.890 --> 01:05:26.500
with an increase of these diseases

01:05:26.500 --> 01:05:29.150
because the clock has been accelerated.

01:05:29.150 --> 01:05:32.250
- So this is, most of our listeners,

01:05:32.250 --> 01:05:34.450
most of the people that
are listening to this,

01:05:34.450 --> 01:05:36.790
watching this are going to be adults

01:05:36.790 --> 01:05:38.990
who're going to be making
decisions for themselves.

01:05:38.990 --> 01:05:39.823
But,

01:05:40.764 --> 01:05:43.590
much of this, if not all of
this is really translatable

01:05:43.590 --> 01:05:45.990
to what parents should be starting

01:05:45.990 --> 01:05:49.233
to help their children develop the habits

01:05:49.233 --> 01:05:51.350
of eating in these patterns as well.

01:05:51.350 --> 01:05:53.780
- Right, and it's okay to be hungry.

01:05:53.780 --> 01:05:54.613
- once in a while.
- Exactly.

